WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:09.200
Batman Vs. Superman is an all-consuming cultural behemoth,

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.700
the purest incarnation of the tent-pole film.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.380
Possibly the last superhero movie of any real cultural importance,

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.460
at least for the next 15 to 20 years,

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.780
until the whole genre's inevitably ressurrected after it's equally inevitable death.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:25.220
Batman VS Superman is inescapable.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.660
A quarter of a billion dollars poured just into production,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.620
with at least another quarter billion spent on marketing in all its flavors.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.580
In many ways, Batman vs Superman is pristine - perfect, even.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.160
The preservation of screen direction is flawless, the continuity of action meticulous -

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.360
Multiple people were employed with the sole job of making sure Henry Cavill's sneer was

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.440
just right every take so that it would cut together without issue.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.480
The computer-generated elements are so flawlessly photorealistic, that you'd be forgiven for not noticing

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.740
that there's more live-action Cinema in Wall-e.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:01:01.200
It is an expertly sculpted puff of air. A dour, exhausting 150 minute ode to creative bankruptcy,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.660
Batman vs Superman is the kind of movie that makes you question your faith in Cinema itself.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:11.120
Why did we ever care? On some levels, its existence functions as a commentary on the nature of audience.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.880
Is this not what we signed up for? A high mass, where we ritualistically recount the exploits

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.140
of untouchable demigods in a detached language, intentionally posturing itself

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.160
above the supplicants in the pews?

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.780
In the beginning, there was darkness,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.440
and then Joe Chill shot Thomas and Martha Wayne. Let us pray.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.180
You may have gathered this already, but I didn't much care for Batman vs Superman- though like I said

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:38.060
in many ways the film is perfect. It polished and buffed to a brooding shine.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.420
It is technically flawless in the way that only a quarter of a billion dollars can buy.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.220
The script is trash, of course, a trifling, joyless tour through plot cul-de-sacs and

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:53.080
actions the characters take - or fail to take - for no internal reason other than to progress the meager plot.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.100
This sounds damning, but in truth it describes far more films than it excludes.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:05.460
It's true failing is spiritual, it is an empty vessel, it is a vast revival tent with nothing inside.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.520
Men &amp; Chicken is the opposite of a cultural behemoth, it is also a deeply flawed film.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.200
A Danish absurdist comedy, there is a good chance that you've never heard of it, and

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.140
an even better chance that you'll never see it, or even possibly have a chance to see it.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.660
Mads Mikkelson rapes a bunch of chickens.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.120
That's not even a terribly important point to the overall structure of the film...

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:27.320
But... It gets your attention.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:32.460
The Lure is a polish electro-Pop strip club musical adaptation of The Little Mermaid.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.260
Nuts is a documentary about the history of medical quackery told mostly through

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:42.360
the lens of a quack doctor's self- mythologizing biography. It is as much about quack medicine and cure-alls

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:47.300
as it is about the way that we, as an audience, interact with the authority of authorship.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:52.300
Lace Crater is a mumblecore horror about a woman who catches an STD from a ghost.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.860
Thematically, it's about the unpredictability of existence and the lack of a storybook arc to our lives.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.900
Under The Shadow is an Iranian horror film about the way that our context shapes and confines us.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.220
This isn't to say that there are some inherently noble dimension to films that

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:12.140
succeed at simply not being Batman vs Superman, even indie underground films are

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.140
still products made by creators, who are generally aware of the context that they exist in.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:21.860
Whether you are Lace Crater or Batman vs Superman, you still need to address fundamental questions of

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:28.680
appeal, of audience, of marketing... The key differences in the answers to these questions, the sense of scope and scale.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.720
Where Snyder and friends come up with a solution of throwing a quarter billion dollars of advertising at the problem,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.980
meaner artists rely on taboo, gimmick and hook.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.100
Luma quarterly, a local online arts magazine, commissioned me to

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:43.120
create a video essay about the Calgary Underground Film Festival.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.440
The topic was left to me to decide, however broad or specific I wanted to make it,

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.140
whether I wanted to talk about something only broadly tangential to the idea of

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:56.200
the festival or if I wanted to drill down deep into the specifics of any one film that I saw.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:00.160
I was given a Press Pass, a collection of trailer stubs, and left to run amok

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:05.160
I mention this because it's the critical context through which I experienced the festival.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:10.260
It is the context that this essay exists within, it is the product of a process where

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.440
I have been asked to make something and within that space I am now confronted with a number of questions.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.760
I would most certainly like to make something that I am personally comfortable attaching my name to,

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:25.480
and to make something that the editors at Luma will feel reflects the tone and depth that they want for the publication.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.220
I would like to make something that my regular audience would engage with, and share.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.340
I want to take the opportunity to push myself, stepping outside the boundaries of the work

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:37.780
I traditionally produce, but not so far that I end up in unfamiliar territory.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:44.880
I am pulled between the competing desire to produce something novel and risky, but also safe, familiar, and appealing.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.220
I lurked around the festival edges for days, I interviewed several of the creators involved with CUFF-cade,

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:56.120
an arcade-style addition of Indie games so the festival as a symbiotic attempt at broadening the umbrella of art

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:00.560
that the festival promotes, and giving the public more ways to engage with the space.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.920
One of the creators is a woman whom I've had several exchanges with before on Twitter,

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.700
none of which were terribly polite on anyone's part.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:12.620
The backstory behind the interactions are so petty and obtuse that it would take an entire essay just to explain,

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:16.920
and the stakes are so low that irrelevant feels like too soft a description.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.460
Nonetheless, the anxiety of that interaction hovered over me for days.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.260
The interview itself was lightweight and cordial, little more than an opportunity for self-promotion.

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:29.580
After publishing an edit of the interviews, I reflected on the puff-piece nature of it and

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:36.100
vowed to come back to the subject later in the week to try and get a meatier bite at the idea of games as an independant art.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.900
I wanted to come back and talk about the way that we interface games with the public,

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.780
the performative aspects of arcades as gaming in the public space,

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:47.900
and how that context changes the way the creators think about their own work. Never did come back to it. I

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.600
I never did come back to it.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:56.780
I took the opportunity afforded by the privilege of a Press Pass to interview several of the festival programmers and volunteers.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:59.480
They were good interviews, but what is there to say, really?

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:06.820
CUFF is a somewhat self-explanatory thing, an opportunity to throw a party, promote some niche works have some fun, and make a few bucks.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:10.500
There are worse reasons to do things, and there's not a lot of hard questions to ask.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:15.000
This is a dimension that has always haunted my attempts to say something about festivals and conventions.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.740
They are commercial ventures in a niche ecosystem, a chance for self-promotion,

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:23.080
a chance to share something salacious with an audience looking to get off the beaten path,

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.260
but it is ultimately mitigated risk.

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:31.060
It is delivering a desired product to a willing audience. It is an act of commerce, not an act of rebellion.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:39.920
"Underground, to me, means shining a spotlight on films that might not otherwise get to be seen so, I see us as a festival that, uh,

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:46.280
is specifically curated to select films that might not otherwise get a wider release,

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:49.800
with a few selections that will bring the crowds"

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.380
This is the context that the festival itself exists in, asking the same questions

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.740
What kind of a product do you want to make and attach your name to?

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:01.220
How do you balance the pressures of novelty and the pressures of appeal?

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:07.720
The week culminated in taking Harrison Atkins, the writer, director, and editor of Lace Crater out to breakfast and recording an hour-long interview

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:14.360
about the philosophy of filmmaking while we ate waffles and omelets. I wanted to talk about the maturing of mumblecore in the sense of

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:18.240
generational anxiety that I've always felt lies at the root of the sub-genre.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:26.280
Mumblecore is an expression of Millennial angst, our crack at Cinema verité, our attempt at capturing something authentic and real.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:31.080
"...there, I think mumblecore corresponds both to like a- to like um, a...

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:36.960
Like a simple like, pragmatic sensibility and also like this like - this ethos, that's sort of um ...

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:46.300
Interested in like a more... um, Naturalistic or emotionally truthful... um, like random storytelling."

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:49.790
The version of this essay built around that idea never materialized.

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:53.480
I began to write it, but quickly self edited it into oblivion.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:59.060
It wasn't good enough, my research wasn't complete enough, my familiarity not comfortable enough. It was stale

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.880
I threw it out because the anxiety at the core of my self-assignment was to

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.880
try and capture something more than just a puff piece promoting a local arts festival.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:10.960
Men &amp; Chickens stars Mads Mikkelson because without the brand power of the name actor,

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.000
the film would never have been made.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:18.880
Deep down I know I'm not giving CUFF enough credit. Maybe I'm undermining myself due to my own perceived deficiencies

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:24.940
I want to tap into something deeper than the surface, some ephemeral real that is beyond simple commercialism,

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:29.540
some active rebellion, and in that I blind myself to what's actually there

00:08:29.540 --> 00:08:37.660
I don't want to perceive myself as the puff piece writer, and that makes me dismissive of things that are easy to explain. This is a flaw.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:41.640
My desk is littered with Dr. Pepper cans promoting Batman vs Superman

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:49.320
Jesse Eisenberg's Lex Luthor stares at me from the side of a dozen empty cans that I haven't bothered to move to the recycling bin I keep not 6 feet away.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:55.320
CUFF sells swag, arranges deals with sponsors and promoters, the booze flows and the concession is cash only,

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:00.640
the subversive, the offbeat, the flawed, the undesirable - these are the products, and they are for sale.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:08.140
In the context of capitalism, is there any act of rebellion, or is it all just making the machines of Commerce work in favor of your interests?

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:13.940
Batman vs Superman spent a quarter of a billion dollars to fill my peripheral vision with endless reminders of the other

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:21.860
quarter of a billion dollars spent making a vapid pay-in to the idea of the audience as willing idiots... Honestly, I had started to believe it.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:28.520
The simplicity of CUFF, the fact that film Festivals aren't some unique synthesis of elements creating some new thing greater than

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:34.420
the sum of its parts isn't important - CUFF may be self-explanatory, it may be exactly what it says on the tin,

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:41.620
that may very well be a strength, there may not be deeper questions to ask about the metaphysical idea of an underground film festival,

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:49.720
because all the important stuff is right there, laid out front. It is a party where movie fans ring in some weird stuff for fans of weird stuff.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:52.360
It's that simple, and that's enough.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:57.080
Underground film is not some strange, unknowable thing - it's movies.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:05.020
Its films made with passion, films made with cynicism, films made with aspirations for greatness and films made out of lazy inertia

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:09.980
There is no mystical quality that is imbued into a film by virtue of being "underground"

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:17.040
in many ways, you could say underground films aren't even special - the difference between mainstream and underground is largely semantic anyway.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:22.760
Independent, underground films are still just films. It is an industry with an audience.

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:25.180
"That question, which is kind of like the commercial question,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:31.720
Has become so much more, like, for granted for me in the wake of Lace Crater, than it was like while I was making it."

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:36.220
Dan: Because now you've got a product that, like-  well, do you sell it, do you... Like, what do you do with it?
Harrison: Right, well that's the thing...

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.820
"It's like, when I was making the movie I wasn't thinking about selling the movie,

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:46.160
I was thinking about making something that adhered to the movie that I saw in my head I wasn't thinking about whether that movie I saw in my head

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:55.020
was something that, um, you know, like a company could come and make money on by exploiting.

00:10:55.020 --> 00:11:00.420
And I don't think Lace Crater's like uncommercial,

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:10.540
I think that there's a lot about it that like hooks audiences in that like art there, it's a little bit high concept which i think is a cool trait

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:26.380
for trying to sell it, but it's like that same kind of question, that same kind of tension between filmmaking that's personal

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:35.620
and filmmaking that's commercial. That's just reverberating in my head a lot, that decision."

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:38.660
Lace Crater is about lost souls, literally and metaphorically.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:43.440
It's a bittersweet film about two people who find mutual comfort and their shared oblivion

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:51.440
It's about small deaths, the death of a relationship, the death of a phase of our lives, we move on and the old version of us dies.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:12:01.320
It's about anxiety, trying to reconcile our private selves with our public faces and the way that we tailor our interactions to craft the self that we imagine others want to see

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:07.040
or maybe it's not about those things at all - maybe those are the themes that I crafted of the raw material of the film,

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:13.480
because that's what I wanted to see up on the screen, the meanings of the films shaped by the context in which I saw them.

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:19.740
Batman vs Superman looms so large over this essay because it looms so very large over the idea of this essay,

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:26.240
I want people to see my work, I want people to be affected by what I do, I want them to take something personal away from it,

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:31.180
I want them to engage, and think, and respond, and they can't do that if they don't see it.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:35.520
Batman vs Superman's tent may be empty, but the tentpole is so very tall

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:42.260
that it can be seen from miles around. It becomes a common reference point, a geographic feature, a shared word.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:48.400
The comfort of that familiarity eases people into the discussion, even as the discussion wanders far afield from it.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:53.140
All creators are beholden to the realities of process and the pragmatics of finding an audience,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.300
Film Festivals like CUFF are part of that pragmatic framework.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:01.800
I have worried about the reach of my work, as much as the programmers of CUFF worried about the draw of their lineup,

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.699
as much as the creators of the films shown worried about finding a venue for their work.

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:12.340
We like to think of creation as something pure, some act unsullied by any concerns external to the creation itself

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:20.640
but it's not - all creation is a sequence of decisions, a series of compromises, and often those answers are dictated by externalities.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:26.780
Let's frame this another way - Warner Brothers was so anxious about ensuring they would find an audience for Batman vs Superman

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:30.400
that they spent a quarter of a billion dollars on promoting the movie.

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:37.640
I spent a lot of time here talking about context, about the themes of context, in the way the context influences creation and decision making,

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:44.140
and the last note of that I want to bring this back around to, is the context in which we engage with art.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:51.460
In a lot of ways I find it difficult to feel animus towards the films that I see at festivals because the festival environment irrevocably changes

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:55.980
the way that I engage with the films. It is a completely different frame of mind.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:14:04.560
When I go to a film at a festival, I am keenly aware of the fact that I, in all likelihood, know very very little about the film itself or the background behind it.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:14.060
That arrangement is inherently challenging, it demands that I bring more of myself to the film, that I exert more effort in keeping up with where the story is going and what it's doing.

00:14:14.060 --> 00:14:23.100
That relationship alters the outcome, flaws and failings are cast in a more charitable light, as experiments that perhaps didn't turn right.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:31.020
Conversely Batman vs Superman cannot escape it's context, it cannot shake it's existence as  an ever-present monolith, with a price tag higher

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:38.680
than the GDP of a small nation. It's flaws in that context suddenly become industrial in scope, and that knowledge colors everything.

00:14:38.680 --> 00:14:44.660
Because in the end, we and what we bring with us is the most important context there is.

